<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">North-West Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as North-West Resistance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North-West Rebellion was a rising in 1885 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> and some First Nations people in the District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-dumont">Gabriel Dumont</a>. After early success at Duck Lake, the rebels were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a> in May 1885; Riel was tried and hanged for treason, a lasting source of division between English and French Canada.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion.jpg" alt="North-West Rebellion" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seargent Grundy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Batoche_Print_by_Seargent_Grundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
